(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({
n: 17, hours: 5.5, title: "Other Scopes in Your Bid: Demo, Grading, Drainage, Hardscape and More",
slides: [

{ t: "cover",
  h: "Other Scopes in Your Bid: Demo, Grading, Drainage, Hardscape and More",
  lead: "Landscape packages carry demo, grading, erosion control, drainage, hardscape, furnishings, lighting, utilities and general conditions. For each: who does it, whether the C-27 covers it, how to take it off and price it, and how to exclude it in writing.",
  objectives: [
    "Spot every non-landscape item on a scope sheet or bid item list and give each a written answer: self-perform, sub, exclude or NIC",
    "Check each scope against the C-27 and know when a C-7, C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13, A or D-subcategory license is needed",
    "Take off and price demo, haul-off, fine grading, import and export with bank, loose and compacted CY",
    "Price BMPs, landscape drainage, mow curb, stabilized DG, boulders and furnishings from real specs",
    "Handle meters, backflow, sleeves, DigAlert and establishment without leaving money on the table",
    "Check your numbers against real Caltrans and City of San Diego line items"
  ],
  time: [["Reading and slides", "2 h"], ["Practice assignments", "3.5 h"], ["Module total", "5.5 h"]],
  body: `<p>Takeoff basics are in <a href="#m7-1">Module 7</a>, labor in <a href="#m9-1">Module 9</a>, proposal format in <a href="#m13-1">Module 13</a>. This module covers deciding, pricing and writing the scopes next to yours.</p>`
},

{ h: "Why other scopes land on you, and the three answers",
  lead: "The landscape sub is the last trade in the dirt, so GCs and agencies push nearby work into its package. Every extra item gets one written answer: self-perform, sub it, or exclude it.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>What lands on you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Private GC scope sheet</td><td>Planter demo, fine grading, area drains, mow curbs, DG paths, benches, lighting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landscape spec sections (CSI Division 32)</td><td>Site furnishings, unit pavers, decomposed granite (DG). Whatever is in “your” section, the GC prices into you.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans bid item list, you as prime</td><td>Every item: traffic control, storm water, clearing and grubbing, excavation, concrete, dike, signs, electrical service</td></tr>
<tr><td>City lump sum (“includes but is not limited to”)</td><td>Prime splits one LS among subs; you must say exactly which parts you cover</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Answer</th><th>Use when</th><th>Write</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Self-perform</td><td>Licensed, equipped, priced from real costs</td><td>Its own line in your scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sub it</td><td>Not your trade, but the customer wants one number; you have a written quote</td><td>“Includes [item] by a licensed [class] subcontractor”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclude</td><td>Not your trade, open-ended risk, no quote by bid day, or another package covers it</td><td>“Excludes [item]; by [GC/others]”</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Test each item: legal under your license? Crew and equipment? Small enough to risk (a $2,000 miss is cheap, a $150,000 wall is not)? Real quote in hand? No quote, no number.</p>`,
  ex: `<p>Caltrans 11-422004 (I-5 Carmel Valley): 56 bid items, about 20 landscape or irrigation. The rest included Remove Concrete (66 CY), Roadway Excavation (340 CY), Hot Mix Asphalt, Pervious Concrete Pavement, 6" perforated underdrain (230 LF) and Minor Concrete (19 CY). Diversified Landscape Co. won at $557,307.25 against three civil contractors.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Silence reads as included. A blank line on a GC form can be read as “included at zero.” Write “Excluded” on it. As a public prime you can't exclude: the bid item list is the contract, so you self-perform or list a sub (<a href="#m14-1">Module 14</a>). That decision starts at go/no-go (<a href="#m3-1">Module 3</a>).</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Item List and Bid Summary (09/09/2021); City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, Section 7-3.1."
},

{ h: "Your license: what the C-27 covers",
  lead: "The C-27 is broad for landscape and grading for landscape. Outside that, use the incidental-and-supplemental rule or a licensed sub.",
  body: `<p><strong>16 CCR §832.27:</strong> “A landscape contractor constructs, maintains, repairs, installs, or subcontracts the development of landscape systems and facilities for public and private gardens and other areas which are designed to aesthetically, architecturally, horticulturally, or functionally improve the grounds within or surrounding a structure or a tract or plot of land. In connection therewith, a landscape contractor prepares and grades plots and areas of land for the installation of any architectural, horticultural and decorative treatment or arrangement.”</p>
<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Clearly inside</h3><ul>
<li>Irrigation, planting, soil prep, mulch</li>
<li>Grading land <em>for</em> landscape</li>
<li>Landscape facilities: DG paths, edging, boulders, drainage serving planting</li>
<li>Subcontracting landscape work</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Gray or outside</h3><ul>
<li>Mass grading, paving, utilities (A, C-12)</li>
<li>Structural concrete, large flatwork (C-8)</li>
<li>Line-voltage electrical (C-10)</li>
<li>Pools (C-53), engineered retaining walls, permitted shade structures</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  deeper: `<p><strong>Incidental and supplemental.</strong> B&amp;P §7059 lets a specialty contractor do other-trade work that is “incidental and supplemental” to its licensed work; 16 CCR §831 defines it as work “essential to accomplish the work in which the contractor is classified.” A short controller conduit trench in your irrigation job may qualify; a concrete plaza does not.</p>
<p><strong>B license.</strong> B&amp;P §7057: a B contractor can't take a contract for one specialty trade (other than framing or carpentry) unless it needs at least two unrelated trades, the B holds that specialty, or subcontracts it to a licensed specialty contractor. The company's B helps on multi-trade work; it doesn't make the landscape division a concrete contractor.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Unsure? Ask CSLB before bid day and file the answer. Work outside your class risks citations and your right to be paid. That's a call for the owner and the company's attorney.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-27 Landscaping classification (16 CCR §832.27): <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cslb.ca.gov</a>; B classification and B&P §7057 text on the CSLB B page; B&P §7059 and 16 CCR §831 as quoted in CSLB's 2023 rulemaking notice (cslb.ca.gov/Resources/LawsAndRegulations/2023)."
},

{ h: "The other licenses you'll meet",
  lead: "When a scope falls outside the C-27, the bid documents, the GC or your sub's license decides who does it.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Name</th><th>CSLB scope (short)</th><th>On landscape jobs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>General Engineering</td><td>Fixed works needing engineering skill: irrigation, drainage, parks, playgrounds, excavating, grading, trenching, paving</td><td>Prime on civil and park jobs; mass grading, storm drain, streets</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-7</td><td>Low Voltage Systems</td><td>Energy-limited systems not over 91 volts; lists “low voltage landscape lighting”</td><td>Low-voltage lighting</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-8</td><td>Concrete</td><td>Mass, pavement, flat and other concrete</td><td>Walks, plazas, curbs, footings, seat walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-10</td><td>Electrical</td><td>Wires, fixtures, raceways, conduits using electrical energy</td><td>Controller power, line-voltage lights</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-12</td><td>Earthwork and Paving</td><td>Cut, fill, excavation, grade, trench, backfill; paving</td><td>Rough grading, import/export, asphalt</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-13</td><td>Fencing</td><td>Fences, railings, game court enclosures, playground game equipment, backstops, gates (not masonry walls)</td><td>Fences, gates, backstops</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-21</td><td>Building Moving/Demolition</td><td>Demolishes and removes structures, including foundations</td><td>Structure removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-29</td><td>Masonry</td><td>Block, brick, stone; bearing and non-bearing walls and fences</td><td>Block walls, veneer, seat walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-42</td><td>Sanitation System</td><td>Septic tanks, storm drains, other drain structures</td><td>Storm drain lines and structures</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-53</td><td>Swimming Pool</td><td>Pools, spas, hot tubs</td><td>Pools and spas (not ordinary fountains)</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-6</td><td>Concrete-Related Services</td><td>Interlocking precast pavers; concrete sawing, breaking, coring, coloring</td><td>Sawcutting, pavers</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-12</td><td>Synthetic Products</td><td>Lists synthetic turf and PVC piping for irrigation and drainage</td><td>Synthetic turf</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-34</td><td>Prefabricated Equipment</td><td>Lists playground equipment and bus stop shelters</td><td>Playground equipment</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-49</td><td>Tree Service</td><td>Prunes and removes trees, limbs, stumps (including grinding), guying</td><td>Tree removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-59</td><td>Hydroseed Spraying</td><td>Seed applied through liquid to surfaces prepared by others</td><td>Hydroseed subs</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-63</td><td>Construction Clean-up</td><td>Removes construction debris from grounds</td><td>Debris haul-off</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Check every sub on CSLB's Instant License Check: right class, active, bond, workers' comp. A wrong-class listed sub is a problem on public work.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB classification pages for A, C-7, C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13, C-21, C-29, C-42, C-53 and C-61 subcategories D-6, D-12, D-34, D-49, D-59, D-63: <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cslb.ca.gov licensing classifications</a> (checked October 2026)."
},

{ h: "Scope responsibility matrix",
  lead: "Who usually does each scope, whether the landscape sub usually carries it, and which license fits. A starting point; the bid documents always win.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Typically by</th><th>Landscape sub?</th><th>License that fits</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Remove existing plants, irrigation, mulch</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clearing and grubbing (light vegetation)</td><td>Grading or landscape</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (for landscape), C-12, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree and stump removal</td><td>Tree service or landscape</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-49; C-27 when part of landscape work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete and asphalt demo, sawcut</td><td>Demo, grading or concrete sub</td><td>Small areas only</td><td>C-12, C-21, D-6, A; C-27 when incidental</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rough grading, cut/fill, import/export</td><td>Grading contractor</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-12, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading of planting areas</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP writing (QSD), BMP inspections (QSP)</td><td>GC or prime, with consultant</td><td>Prime: yes; sub: no</td><td>QSD/QSP certificates, not a license class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiber rolls, hydroseed, erosion blanket</td><td>Landscape or erosion control sub</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27, D-59 (hydroseed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area drains in planting areas</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (landscape drainage)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm drain pipe, catch basins, structures</td><td>Civil contractor</td><td>Rarely</td><td>A, C-42, C-34</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention soil media and planting</td><td>Civil for structure, landscape for soil and plants</td><td>Planting yes; basin often no</td><td>A for basin; C-27 for planting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete walks, plazas, curbs</td><td>Concrete sub</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-8</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mow curb, edging, header board</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pavers</td><td>Paver or masonry sub</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-6, C-29, C-27 in landscape</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG paving, boulders, cobble, stepping stones</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retaining walls, structural seat walls</td><td>Concrete or masonry sub</td><td>Low garden walls only</td><td>C-8, C-29 (engineered design)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Benches, trash cans, bike racks</td><td>Landscape or concrete sub</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 when part of landscape; D-34</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playground equipment, safety surfacing</td><td>Playground installer</td><td>Rarely</td><td>D-34, C-13 (game equipment), A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fencing and gates</td><td>Fence sub</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-13</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shade structures</td><td>GC or specialty</td><td>No</td><td>B, plus building permit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controller power, line-voltage lights</td><td>Electrical sub</td><td>No (sub it)</td><td>C-10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low-voltage landscape lighting</td><td>Lighting or landscape</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>C-7 (listed by name); C-10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Synthetic turf</td><td>Turf installer</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-12 (listed by name); C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water meter, service line to POC</td><td>Water agency, plumbing or civil</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Agency; C-36, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow preventer and enclosure</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (irrigation), C-36</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves under paving</td><td>Landscape (before paving)</td><td>Usually</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plant establishment, maintenance</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Traffic control, mobilization, sweeping</td><td>Prime</td><td>Only when you're prime</td><td>Traffic control sub as needed</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>“Typically” varies by GC, agency and region. One GC puts area drains with plumbing, the next with landscape. When two packages seem to include an item, ask.</p>`,
  src: "License classes: CSLB classification pages (cslb.ca.gov). The “typically by” column is general industry practice; confirm on every job."
},

{ h: "Demo: types, units and demo buried in other items",
  lead: "Demo is either its own bid item or buried inside another one. The buried kind is where you lose money.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Includes</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Remove existing landscape</td><td>Plants, turf, mulch, edging</td><td>SF or LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remove irrigation</td><td>Heads, valves, controllers, pipe (or abandon in place if allowed)</td><td>LS, EA, LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clearing and grubbing</td><td>Vegetation, roots, stumps, debris before grading</td><td>LS, SF or acre</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadside clearing (Caltrans)</td><td>Mow, trash and debris, weed control along the work</td><td>LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree and stump removal</td><td>Cut, haul, grind below grade</td><td>EA by trunk size</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete and asphalt demo</td><td>Sawcut, break, load, haul</td><td>SF, SY or CY; sawcut LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Salvage</td><td>Remove carefully, deliver to owner in usable condition</td><td>EA or LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protect in place</td><td>Trees, utilities, paving kept undamaged</td><td>LS or EA; fence LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Haul-off and disposal</td><td>Trucking plus dump or recycling fees</td><td>Loads, tons or CY</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>“Remove” on Caltrans plans includes disposal; “salvage” means deliver it; “abandon” means leave it, often capped or filled. Read the note, not just the symbol.</p>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Paid items:</strong> Remove Irrigation Facility and Salvage Irrigation Facility (11-428704 items 34, 35, LS); Remove Concrete (CY) 66 CY (11-422004 item 16); Remove Asphalt Concrete Dike 330 LF (11-428804 item 83); Remove Chain Link Fence 10 LF (11-428804 item 88); Remove Retaining Wall (LS) (11-4C0034 item 56).</p>
<p><strong>Buried:</strong> City K-24-1952 (EMTS Boat Dock Esplanade, bid December 6, 2023, estimate $1,600,000), 300-1.1: clearing and grubbing “shall also include saw cutting, demolition, removal and disposal of all existing improvements called out on the Plans to be removed,” including “excess soil, pavement, concrete pavers, concrete ribbon gutter, concrete mow curb, curb and base course, sidewalk to nearest joint, curb and gutter… signs and sign posts, vegetation.” 300-1.4: paid inside the lump sum “Construction of Park Improvements.” No separate payment.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>The same section tells the bidder to “inspect the project site to verify the magnitude and cost of all Clearing and Grubbing.” That puts the risk on you. Walk, photograph and measure. As a sub, ask the prime in writing which removals are yours. Search specs for “remove,” “clearing and grubbing” and “in conflict with the work.”</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Item Lists: 11-428704, 11-422004, 11-428804, 11-4C0034. City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A Supplementary Special Provisions 300-1.1 item 10 and 300-1.4 items 3–4; Notice Inviting Bids items 3 and 6."
},

{ h: "Demo takeoff and pricing: a 600 SF patio",
  lead: "Convert what comes out into the units that drive cost: in-place CY, loose CY, tons, loads and crew days.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Measure</td><td>Paving by SF with thickness (plans, site visit or core); sawcut LF along the edge that stays; trees by count and trunk size</td></tr>
<tr><td>In-place CY</td><td>SF × inches ÷ 324</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loose CY</td><td>In-place CY × swell (ask your hauler)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tons</td><td>Concrete 145–150 lb/CF; 150 × 27 = 4,050 lb ≈ 2 tons per in-place CY</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loads</td><td>Loose CY ÷ truck capacity, round up; weight limits can cap concrete loads first</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example (example rates; get real hauler, recycler and sawcut quotes)",
  ex: `<p>600 SF × 4" ÷ 324 = <strong>7.41 CY</strong> in place; × 1.50 swell = 11.1 LCY → <strong>2 loads</strong> at 10 LCY; 7.41 × 2.0 = <strong>14.8 tons</strong>; sawcut 120 LF.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sawcut sub</td><td>120 LF × $4.00</td><td class="num">$480.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skid steer with breaker</td><td>1 day</td><td class="num">$450.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator + laborer</td><td>16 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trucking</td><td>2 loads × 2.5 hr × $130</td><td class="num">$650.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recycler</td><td>14.8 tons × $60</td><td class="num">$888.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>$5.07/SF; $410.80/in-place CY</td><td class="num"><strong>$3,044.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Real check: Remove Concrete (CY), 66 CY on 11-422004, drew $200, $200, $240 and $342/CY. Small jobs cost more per CY because the sawcut trip, rental day and truck minimums don't shrink.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Hidden thickness: a “4-inch” patio can be 6 inches at the edges, reinforced, or sit on a second slab. State assumed thickness and a unit price for extra. Green waste, clean concrete, clean soil and mixed debris go to different facilities at different rates; recyclers usually beat landfills. Call for current rates every bid.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Summary, item 16 (Remove Concrete (CY)), unit prices of all four bidders. $36.00 labor hour from the Module 9 practice crew."
},

{ h: "Demo risks, protection and exclusions",
  lead: "The costliest demo mistakes are often damage to things that stay and contamination nobody priced. Price what you see; write down what you assumed.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Handle it by</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Thicker concrete, rebar, buried slabs</td><td>Assumed thickness in writing; unit price for extra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buried debris and rock in planting areas</td><td>Exclude, or unit price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hazardous materials (lead, asbestos, contaminated soil)</td><td>Always exclude</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilities found during demo</td><td>DigAlert, potholing, state who repairs unmarked lines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dump fees rise before the work</td><td>Price-validity date; check rates before bid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage to protected trees and paving</td><td>Pre-work photos; price protection fence, upkeep and slower work near it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trees: permits, nesting birds, protected species</td><td>Size groups (6–12", 12–24", over 24"); tree service (D-49) quote for big or risky trees</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Proposal wording:</strong> “Includes removal of the existing 4-inch concrete patio (approx. 600 SF) with sawcut at the walk to remain. Thicker concrete, reinforcing or buried slabs at $___ per SF additional.” “Excludes demolition of paving, curbs, walls, structures and utilities not listed above.” “Excludes removal of buried debris, rock over 6 inches and contaminated or hazardous materials.”</p>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Lead soil:</strong> 11-428804 and 11-430674 each had “Roadway Excavation (Type Z-2) (Aerially Deposited Lead).” On 11-430674 it drew $229.80–$422.00/CY vs. $22.00–$60.30/CY for ordinary Roadway Excavation: about ten times more.</p>
<p><strong>Protection and salvage:</strong> 11-428704 item 19 Temporary Protection Fence (Rope), 1,120 LF: posts at most 10 ft apart, embedded at least 24", yellow 3/8" polypropylene rope tied with a clove hitch, damaged parts replaced. Bids $0.37–$10.00/LF. Salvaged irrigation went to the Caltrans Kearny Mesa Landscape Office, 7181 Opportunity Road, with 2 business days' notice; Salvage Irrigation Facility drew $500–$10,000.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Specs often make you replace a damaged protected tree, sometimes at a large box size or an arborist's value. One mature tree can cost more than the whole protection item.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-430674 Bid Summary items 26 and 27; 11-428804 items 27 and 28; 11-428704 Special Provisions 16-2.06 and 15-1.03C; 11-428704 Bid Summary items 19 and 35; CSLB D-49 Tree Service classification."
},

{ h: "Rough vs. fine grading, and who sets the grades",
  lead: "The grading contractor delivers rough grade; you take planting areas to finish grade. State the hand-off tolerance in every proposal.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Rough (mass) grading</h3><ul>
<li>Cut and fill to the grading plan; dozers, scrapers, rollers</li>
<li>Compaction tested by the soils engineer</li>
<li>C-12 or A contractor</li>
<li>Leaves planting areas at rough grade within a tolerance</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Fine (finish) grading</h3><ul>
<li>Planting areas to finish grade, smooth and draining</li>
<li>Skid steer, small tractor, rakes; round slope tops and toes, remove clods and rock</li>
<li>C-27 (grading <em>for</em> landscape)</li>
<li>Sets soil below walks for mulch</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hand-off clause:</strong> “Rough grade delivered to within ±0.1 ft of finish grade, less the depth of soil amendments and mulch, by others.” (0.1 ft = 1.2".) Check grades with a laser before you accept an area; send highs and lows to the GC.</li>
<li><strong>Grading plan (civil):</strong> spot elevations, contours, slopes; FG finish grade, FS finish surface, TC top of curb, FL flow line. <strong>Staking:</strong> surveyor's cut (C) and fill (F) marks; usually GC-paid on private jobs. If the form says “staking by landscape,” price a surveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Details:</strong> finish soil below hardscape, e.g. 1" in turf and 3" in mulched areas. Use your job's numbers.</li>
<li><strong>Ripping and incorporating:</strong> mass-graded pads leave planting areas compacted. Rip (cross-rip doubles passes), then till amendments to spec depth; screen rock over a set size. Ask the GC whether planting areas are left at pad compaction.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>K-24-1952, 801-2.3: “Planting surfaces shall be graded with no less than one percent (1%) surface slope for positive drainage,” undulations “leveled and floated out before planting,” and “Final grades shall be acceptable to the Resident Engineer before planting operations will be allowed to begin.” That is an inspection hold. Caltrans 11-4C0034 (bid May 11, 2023) item 52 Incorporate Materials, 73,800 SQFT, low bid $0.20/SQFT ($14,760); item 51 Compost, 120 CY at $70.00/CY. Material and mixing were separate items.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>No elevations in planting areas and nobody staking? Send an RFI (<a href="#m12-1">Module 12</a>). Never set commercial grades by eye; ponding at a building is your callback.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 801-2.3 Finish Grading, items 5–9; Caltrans 11-4C0034 Bid Summary (Contract Proposal of Low Bidder), items 51 and 52."
},

{ h: "Bank, loose and compacted CY, and the high rough grade",
  lead: "Soil swells when dug and shrinks when compacted. Haul in loose CY, buy fill from bank CY, and place in compacted CY.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bank CY (BCY)</td><td>In place, before digging</td><td>Cut quantities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loose CY (LCY)</td><td>Dug and fluffed</td><td>Trucks, haul and dump fees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compacted CY (CCY)</td><td>Placed and compacted</td><td>Fill quantities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swell factor</td><td>LCY ÷ BCY (above 1.00)</td><td>LCY = BCY × swell</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shrink factor</td><td>CCY ÷ BCY (usually below 1.00)</td><td>BCY needed = CCY ÷ shrink</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Import loads</strong> = (CCY ÷ shrink) × swell ÷ truck CY, round up. Example (swell 1.25, shrink 0.90): 200 CCY → 222.2 BCY → 277.8 LCY → 28 loads. Ordering 200 CY leaves you about 78 LCY (8 loads) short.</p>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · rough grade comes in high (example rates)",
  ex: `<p>8,000 SF shrub area needs soil 3" below walk for mulch; the grader left it at walk height.</p>
<p>8,000 × 3 ÷ 324 = <strong>74.1 BCY</strong>; × 1.25 = 92.6 LCY → <strong>10 loads</strong> at 10 LCY.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Trucking</td><td>10 × 2 hr × $130</td><td class="num">$2,600.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clean soil disposal</td><td>10 × $100</td><td class="num">$1,000.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skid steer</td><td>2 days × $350</td><td class="num">$700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator + laborer</td><td>32 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$1,152.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>$0.68/SF never in the landscape price</td><td class="num"><strong>$5,452.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>With the ±0.1 ft clause, this is a change order. Without it, the GC calls it your fine grading. One sentence decides who pays $5,452.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Swell and shrink vary widely by soil (sand, clay, rock). Treat 1.25/0.90 as placeholders; get factors from the soils report or grading sub. Amendments raise grade too: 6 CY of compost per 1,000 SF tilled in adds volume to export.</p>`
},

{ h: "Import, export, compaction, testing and real excavation prices",
  lead: "Soil in or out costs trucking, fees and tests. Compacted surfaces cost lab tests. Small quantities cost far more per CY.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Import:</strong> spec usually requires tested or sample-approved topsoil. Price material (CY or ton), trucking, spreading, testing; get the supplier's test report first.</li>
<li><strong>Export:</strong> clean soil to a fill site or another job; mixed or contaminated soil costs much more; some sites require tests before accepting loads.</li>
<li><strong>Compaction:</strong> percent of lab maximum density (relative compaction). Planting soil stays loose; paving base, wall backfill, boulder bases and DG subgrade get compacted. Who pays for tests varies: owner's lab on many private jobs, but retests after a failure are yours.</li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Real Caltrans item</th><th>Job</th><th>Quantity</th><th class="num">Bids per CY</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-422004</td><td>340 CY</td><td class="num">$75.00 – $165.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-428804</td><td>3,620 CY</td><td class="num">$20.00 – $90.10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-430674</td><td>6,480 CY</td><td class="num">$22.00 – $60.30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-4C0034 (low bid)</td><td>180 CY</td><td class="num">$125.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structure Excavation / Backfill</td><td>11-4C0034 (low bid)</td><td>156 / 61 CY</td><td class="num">$35.00 / $90.00</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>On 11-422004 the landscape winner carried Roadway Excavation at $93.30/CY; the second bidder (a general engineering firm) listed an excavation sub for 93% of that item.</p>
<p><strong>Proposal wording:</strong> “Includes fine grading of planting areas to finish grade per L-series details.” “Excludes rough grading, cut and fill, import and export of soil, and compaction testing. Rough grade delivered to ±0.1 ft of finish grade, less depth of amendments and mulch, by others.” “Excludes construction staking.” “Import of planting soil, if required, at $___ per CY placed.”</p>`,
  ex: `<p>Caltrans rock mulch (11-422004, 20-5.03E) and boulder subgrade (20-5.06C): “not less than 90 percent relative compaction.” City stabilized DG (K-24-1952, 200-5.3.3): subgrade at least 90% per ASTM D1557; 200-5.3.4 requires written tests “by a certified testing laboratory” for each lift, “the sole financial responsibility of the Contractor.”</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>The A class lists “land leveling and earthmoving projects, excavating, grading, trenching, paving and surfacing work.” The C-12 covers earth for “a cut, fill, excavation, grade, trench, backfill.” The C-27 covers grading “for the installation of any architectural, horticultural and decorative treatment.” Grading that serves the landscape fits the C-27; a building pad or street doesn't.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries: 11-422004 item 17 and subcontractor list; 11-428804 item 27; 11-430674 item 26; 11-4C0034 items 15–17. 11-422004 Special Provisions 20-5.03E(3) and 20-5.06C; City of San Diego K-24-1952 200-5.1.1, 200-5.3.2, 200-5.3.3, 200-5.3.4. CSLB A, C-12 and C-27 classification pages."
},

{ h: "Storm water and dust: SWPPP, WPCP and APCD Rule 55",
  lead: "Certified people write and run the storm water plan; crews install and maintain BMPs. As a sub, own only the BMPs, in writing.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>CGP (Construction General Permit)</td><td>State Water Board Order 2022-0057-DWQ, in effect since September 1, 2023. Applies to 1 acre or more of disturbance, or less if part of a larger common plan of 1 acre or more.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP (Storm Water Pollution Prevention Plan)</td><td>The written plan for a CGP project</td></tr>
<tr><td>QSD / QSP</td><td>Qualified SWPPP Developer writes and revises the SWPPP / Qualified SWPPP Practitioner inspects and runs BMPs in the field</td></tr>
<tr><td>WPCP</td><td>Water Pollution Control Program (Caltrans) or Plan (City) for jobs under the CGP threshold</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMP (Best Management Practice)</td><td>One control: fiber roll, inlet protection, etc.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk level 1–3</td><td>Higher risk, more inspections, sampling and reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>REAP (Rain Event Action Plan)</td><td>Prepared before forecast rain on higher-risk sites</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>San Diego County APCD Rule 55 (Fugitive Dust):</strong> no visible dust beyond the property line for more than 3 minutes in any 60. Minimize track-out (grates, gravel beds, wheel washing, tarps) and remove it at the end of each work day. Sweepers must be PM10-efficient, certified to South Coast AQMD Rule 1186; no leaf blowers for track-out.</p>`,
  ex: `<p>11-428704: “This project's risk level is 2”; “You must assign a QSD to develop and revise the SWPPP”; the WPC manager must meet CGP QSP requirements and hold an 8-hour Caltrans WPC manager certificate. K-24-1952 is “subject to SWPPP Risk Level 1,” with BMP maintenance documented “by the QSP or QSD.” Street Sweeping (LS): 11-422004 $2,450–$16,500; 11-428704 $2,840–$25,000; 11-430674 $3,000–$60,000.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>As a GC's sub, write: “Includes installation and maintenance of the BMPs shown on sheet EC-1 for [6] months. Excludes SWPPP preparation, QSD/QSP services, sampling, reporting, SMARTS filings and fines.” Also: “Dust control, water truck and street sweeping by GC except during our own soil work.” As a Caltrans prime the whole SWPPP is yours: line up a QSD/QSP consultant before bid day.</p>`,
  src: "State Water Resources Control Board, Construction Stormwater Program: <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/water_issues/programs/stormwater/construction.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waterboards.ca.gov</a> (checked October 2026); 11-428704 Special Provisions 13-3.01A, 13-1.01D(4)(b), 13-3.01C(2)(a); City K-24-1952 1001-1 and 1001-2.10. San Diego County APCD Rule 55 (adopted June 24, 2009), sections (a) and (d): <a href=\"https://www.sdapcd.org/content/dam/sdapcd/documents/rules/current-rules/Rule-55.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sdapcd.org</a>; Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-422004 #12, 11-428704 #17, 11-430674 #22."
},

{ h: "BMP prices and a small BMP package",
  lead: "Take BMPs off the erosion control plan by LF, EA or SF. Upkeep, not install, is where BMP prices lose money.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>BMP</th><th>Unit</th><th>Real bids (job, item)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Temporary fiber roll</td><td>LF</td><td>$1.50–$7.25 (11-431064 #17, 23,800 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary silt fence</td><td>LF</td><td>$2.30–$9.00 (11-422004 #11, 1,840 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary drainage inlet protection</td><td>EA</td><td>$150–$700 (11-428804 #18, 43 EA)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary construction entrance</td><td>EA</td><td>$2,000–$7,546 (11-430674 #21, 9 EA)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary check dam</td><td>LF</td><td>$3.80–$13.34 (11-430674 #19, 760 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary concrete washout</td><td>LS</td><td>$1,000–$20,359 (11-430674 #23)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydroseed</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.10–$0.11 (11-431064 #63, 797,000 SQFT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bonded fiber matrix</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.25–$1.15 (11-422004 #40, 47,600 SQFT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rolled erosion control product (jute mesh)</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.30–$2.00 (11-428804 #79, 45,500 SQFT)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>On 11-428704, 11-428804, 11-430674 and 11-431064 every bidder carried Rain Event Action Plan at $500 each and Storm Water Annual Report at $2,000 each. Identical prices across bidders mean the agency set them. Find these in the special provisions before pricing.</p>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · 2.5-acre site, 6 months (example prices and rates)",
  ex: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fiber rolls, 25-ft</td><td>1,200 LF ÷ 25 = 48 × $40</td><td class="num">$1,920.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stakes, 1 per 4 ft</td><td>300 × $1.00</td><td class="num">$300.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Install labor, 0.02 LH/LF</td><td>24 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$864.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inlet protection</td><td>6 × ($60 + 1 LH × $36)</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Post-rain maintenance</td><td>8 visits × 2 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement, 20% of rolls</td><td>$1,920 × 0.20</td><td class="num">$384.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td></td><td class="num"><strong>$4,620.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Fiber roll: ($1,920 + 300 + 864 + 384) ÷ 1,200 = <strong>$2.89/LF</strong>, inside the real $1.50–$7.25 range. Maintenance and replacement are 21% ($960) of the package.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>BMPs stay until the area is stabilized, often for months. Price post-rain inspections, crushed rolls, sediment cleanout and final removal.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries, item numbers as listed (all bidders' unit prices)."
},

{ h: "Drainage and bioretention",
  lead: "Landscape drainage moves water out of planters. Civil drainage moves water off the site. Price the first; exclude or sub the second.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Unit</th><th>Usually by</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Area drain (grated inlet in planter or lawn)</td><td>EA + LF pipe</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td></tr>
<tr><td>French drain / subdrain (gravel, perforated pipe, fabric)</td><td>LF</td><td>Landscape or civil</td></tr>
<tr><td>Downspout connection</td><td>EA</td><td>Plumbing or landscape</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wall drain</td><td>LF</td><td>Wall contractor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catch basin, curb outlet, parkway drain</td><td>EA</td><td>Civil or concrete</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm drain main</td><td>LF</td><td>Civil (A, C-42)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention / biofiltration basin</td><td>SF, CY media</td><td>Civil builds, landscape plants</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>LID (Low Impact Development)</strong> sites treat rain where it falls. A bioretention basin is layered: mulch or none, engineered soil media (sand, compost, soil blend with a required infiltration rate, made to order with test reports), choking layer, gravel, often an underdrain and overflow, and a liner where infiltration isn't allowed. Specs may forbid fertilizer or floating mulch.</p>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · 6 planter drains to a civil curb outlet (example prices)",
  ex: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Area drains with grates</td><td>6 × $45</td><td class="num">$270.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>4" pipe + 10% fittings</td><td>180 LF × $2.50 = $450 + $45</td><td class="num">$495.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cleanouts</td><td>2 × $35</td><td class="num">$70.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor</td><td>Trench 180 × 0.08 = 14.4 LH; drains 6, cleanouts 1, flush test 2 = 23.4 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$842.40</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trencher</td><td>1 day</td><td class="num">$250.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost before tax</strong></td><td>$321.23 per drain</td><td class="num"><strong>$1,927.40</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Check fall first: at 1% (check the detail), 180 LF needs 1.8" of drop. Flat planters and a high outlet mean the pipe can't drain. Real: 11-422004 item 51, 6" perforated underdrain, 230 LF, bids $12.80, $25.00, $25.00, $28.00/LF; the landscape winner was lowest.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Pipe that ties to the public storm drain, crosses a street, or is part of the engineered storm water system is civil work with inspection, possibly A or C-42. Basin money-losers: media failing the infiltration test, site sediment washing into a finished basin, bottom out of tolerance. Write: “Basin excavation, liner, underdrain, gravel layers and overflow structures by others. Includes furnishing and placing [engineered soil media] and planting per L-series. Protection of completed basins from construction runoff by GC.”</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>The City of San Diego Storm Water Standards (Part 1, BMP Design Manual) require retaining the design storm on site where feasible and biofiltration meeting its standards where it isn't. Build exactly what the detail shows. Check the current edition on <a href=\"https://www.sandiego.gov/sites/default/files/sws_part_1_nov_2017_edition.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sandiego.gov</a> (November 2017 edition shown).</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Item List and Bid Summary, items 45, 50, 51; City of San Diego Storm Water Standards, Part 1: BMP Design Manual (November 2017 edition), sandiego.gov."
},

{ h: "Hardscape split and a concrete mow curb",
  lead: "Edging, mow curb, DG, boulders and stepping stones are landscape. Walks, plazas and structural walls are concrete or masonry: sub them or exclude them.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Usually</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Concrete walks, plazas, driveways</td><td>Concrete sub (C-8)</td><td>Exclude unless you sub it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete mow curb, band, edge</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Small pours, often extruded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel or aluminum edging, header board</td><td>Landscape</td><td>LF per detail</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG, plain or stabilized</td><td>Landscape</td><td>SF and CY; tests on stabilized</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interlocking pavers</td><td>Paver sub (D-6) or landscape</td><td>Base, sand, edge restraint</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stone or block seat walls</td><td>C-29 or C-8</td><td>Footings, rebar, caps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retaining walls</td><td>Concrete or masonry sub, engineered</td><td>Exclude or sub</td></tr>
<tr><td>Boulders, cobble, stepping stones</td><td>Landscape</td><td>EA and SF</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · 400 LF of 6\" × 6\" mow curb (example prices)",
  ex: `<p>0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25 SF × 400 = 100 CF ÷ 27 = <strong>3.70 CY</strong>; +10% = 4.07; order 4.5 CY. One #3 bar: 400 ÷ 20 = 20 + 2 laps = 22 bars.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ready-mix delivered</td><td>4.5 × $200</td><td class="num">$900.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short-load fee</td><td>1 load</td><td class="num">$150.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rebar</td><td>22 × $8.00</td><td class="num">$176.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Form, place, finish, joints</td><td>400 × 0.06 = 24 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$864.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>$5.23/LF</td><td class="num"><strong>$2,090.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Real edging range (products, not poured concrete): Caltrans Edging 11-431064, 6,200 LF, $7.30, $10.00, $10.00/LF; 11-430674, 3,660 LF, $3.60–$10.30/LF.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Concrete minimums dominate small pours: short-load fees, a pump if the truck can't reach, a finisher's half day for 20 LF. Group pours. If the scope sheet lumps “all site concrete” into landscape, call the GC; sub it at markup or exclude it.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>C-8 covers “mass, pavement, flat and other concrete work.” D-6 lists “interlocking precast pavers” and concrete sawing and breaking. C-29 covers block, brick and stone walls and fences “with or without mortar.” Low garden walls and edging fit the C-27's landscape facilities; structural walls don't.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-8, C-29 and C-61/D-6 classification pages (cslb.ca.gov); Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-431064 item 36 and 11-430674 item 47."
},

{ h: "Stabilized DG, pavers and stepping stones",
  lead: "Read the DG spec before you price. Two lifts, a binder minimum and a lab test per lift can double a plain-DG unit price.",
  body: `<p><strong>City K-24-1952, Section 200-5 Stabilized DG:</strong> soil sterilant mixed into the top 2–4" of subgrade; subgrade at least 90%; full-depth edging; DG pre-blended with organic binder, “not less than of twelve (12) pounds of Organic Binder per one (1) ton,” and drop-spreading or roto-tilling binder “is not acceptable”; placed “in two (2) equal two-inch (2") lifts”; surface within 1/8" in 10 ft and accessible; compaction test by a certified lab each lift at contractor's cost.</p>
<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Pavers</h3><ul>
<li>Area by pattern; base CY, bedding sand CY, edge restraint LF</li>
<li>Curves add cut labor and waste</li>
<li>Permeable pavers add open-graded layers and often an underdrain</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Stepping stones</h3><ul>
<li>EA by size; sand or DG bed per detail</li>
<li>Flush or 1/2" above grade per detail</li>
<li>Heavy flagstone needs two workers or a machine</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · 1,500 SF path, 4\" in two lifts, 200 LF steel edging (example prices)",
  ex: `<p>1,500 × 4 ÷ 324 = <strong>18.5 CY</strong> compacted; +20% = 22.2 CY loose; × 1.4 tons/CY (supplier) = <strong>31.1 tons</strong>; binder minimum 31.1 × 12 = <strong>373 lb</strong>; 2 lifts = 2 test visits.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pre-blended stabilized DG</td><td>31.1 × $95/ton</td><td class="num">$2,954.50</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel edging</td><td>200 × $4.50</td><td class="num">$900.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prep, sterilant, place, roll</td><td>1,500 × 0.05 = 75 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$2,700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roller</td><td>2 days × $200</td><td class="num">$400.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compaction tests</td><td>2 × $350</td><td class="num">$700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>$5.10/SF</td><td class="num"><strong>$7,654.50</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Plain DG on Caltrans 11-430674, 55,900 SQFT, drew $1.70–$3.00/SQFT. The small stabilized path above is about twice the top bid. Never copy a big-job plain-DG price onto a small spec path.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 200-5.3.2, 200-5.3.3 and 200-5.3.4; Caltrans 11-430674 Bid Summary item 45."
},

{ h: "Walls and boulders",
  lead: "Seat walls can fit a landscape scope; retaining walls need engineering, permits and a C-8 or C-29. Boulders are landscape, but they are heavy-equipment work with approvals.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Seat and low garden walls</h3><ul>
<li>Decorative; little soil retained</li>
<li>Footing, rebar and cap per detail; CMU with veneer or cast concrete</li>
<li>Take off LF and height, footing CY, block count, cap LF</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Retaining walls</h3><ul>
<li>Engineer-designed; taller or surcharged walls need permits</li>
<li>Wall drains, waterproofing, compacted backfill, inspections</li>
<li>C-8 or C-29: sub or exclude</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p><strong>Boulders:</strong> count by size group (<a href="#m7-1">Module 7</a>); price the stone, delivery, a machine that can lift it, rigging, approval time and a compacted base. Twice the length is about eight times the weight; get weights from the quarry and check lift capacity.</p>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Wall, 11-4C0034</strong> (San Ysidro gateway, bid May 11, 2023; license Class A or a combination of C-8, C-10 and C-27 making up the majority of the work): items 53–55 Structural Concrete, Wall 175 CY, Concrete Surface Texture 600 SQFT, Bar Reinforcing Steel 11,350 LB. Low bid carried wall concrete at $2,200/CY: $385,000 of $1,597,008.50. Diversified Landscape Co., bidding as prime, listed Hazard Construction for 100% of items 53–55; Hazard won as prime and listed Marina Landscape for the landscape items.</p>
<p><strong>Boulders, 11-422004, 20-5.06:</strong> source and front, back and side photos of each boulder 30 days before delivery; tan, rounded, no blast holes, from named quarries “or equal”; sizes A (5×4×3 ft) to D (2×2×1 ft); location and orientation authorized 5 business days before setting; subgrade 90%; bury about 1/3 of height. 20 Decorative Boulders (item 19) drew $320, $330, $620 and $1,250 each.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Wall permit rules vary by city, height and load. Wall without structural details? RFI. Any wall in your proposal: name the drawing, height and detail you priced.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-4C0034 Special Provisions (Notice to Bidders, license requirement) and Bid Summary pages 1, 4 and S1–S2 (subcontractor lists); 11-422004 Special Provisions 20-5.06A–20-5.06C and Bid Summary item 19; CSLB C-8 and C-29 classification pages."
},

{ h: "Furnishings, playgrounds, fencing, shade and water features",
  lead: "Install furnishings from the detail and a written supplier quote. Playgrounds, fences, shade structures, pools and fountains are specialty work most landscape subs exclude or sub.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Who / license</th><th>What to price or watch</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Benches, receptacles, bike racks, tables</td><td>Landscape (C-27 when part of landscape); D-34 lists playground equipment and bus shelters</td><td>Mount: embedded footing, surface bolt to a pad, freestanding. Who pours the pad. Lead time, out-of-state freight, anchors included?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playground equipment</td><td>Maker's certified installer; D-34, C-13 (game equipment)</td><td>Exclude or sub</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safety surfacing</td><td>Surfacing specialist</td><td>Depth and fall heights set by design</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fencing and gates</td><td>C-13 (not masonry walls)</td><td>Exclude or sub</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ornamental metal railings, gates</td><td>C-23 or C-13</td><td>Shop drawings, welding, finish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shade structures, pergolas</td><td>B or specialty, engineered</td><td>Building permit, footings, inspections</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pools and spas</td><td>C-53</td><td>Exclude</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fountains, recirculating features</td><td>Fountain specialist; C-10 for power</td><td>Pumps, basin, filtration, permit; leaks show up after you leave</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Pick one on every furnishing: “Furnish and install,” “Install only, owner-furnished,” or “Excluded.” On a water feature you exclude, you still carry boulders, planting and irrigation around it and sleeves for its pipe and power.</p>`,
  ex: `<p>K-24-1952: 206-9 Site Furnishings (picnic table, bench, litter and recycling receptacle, dog clean-up station, ADA route guide sign), 201-12 Precast Concrete Fence (2016 California Building Code, panels and posts at 4,500 psi) and 206-8 Service Access Gate, all paid inside the LS “Construction of Park Improvements” with walkways, irrigation, planting and lighting (7-3.1). A landscape quote must say which it covers.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>Cal. Health &amp; Safety Code §115725: new public playgrounds must meet ASTM playground standards and U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission guidelines, and operators must have an NPSI-certified playground safety inspector do an initial inspection. Hence the maker's installer and “playground equipment and safety surfacing by others.” If a contract is mostly furnishings, ask CSLB which class fits.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Water feature wording: “Water feature construction, pumps, filtration, waterproofing and electrical excluded. Includes boulders, planting and irrigation around the feature per L-series.” A one-year warranty on a leaking basin can cost more than the item.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 206-9, 201-12, 206-8 and 7-3.1; Cal. Health & Safety Code §115725 (text checked at california.public.law); CSLB C-13, C-23, C-53, C-10 and C-61/D-34 classification pages."
},

{ h: "Lighting, electrical and synthetic turf",
  lead: "Line voltage is C-10 work. Low-voltage landscape lighting is listed by name under the C-7, and synthetic turf under D-12. Plan to sub electrical even as prime.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Line voltage (120V and up)</h3><ul>
<li>Controller power, pole lights, bollards, 120V/277V uplights</li>
<li>Conduit, pull boxes, wire, panels; permit and inspection</li>
<li>C-10 sub, or exclude</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Low voltage (energy-limited)</h3><ul>
<li>12V lighting off a transformer; feeding the transformer is C-10 work</li>
<li>C-7 lists “low voltage landscape lighting”; the C-27 text doesn't mention lighting</li>
<li>Ask CSLB before self-performing</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p><strong>Synthetic turf:</strong> subgrade, optional geotextile, 3–4" aggregate base, leveling layer, turf, seams, infill (lb/SF from the maker). Base CY = SF × inches ÷ 324. Example: 3,000 SF on 4" = 37.0 CY compacted; +25% = 46.3 CY to order; turf +8% waste = 3,240 SF. Sports fields add engineered drainage, shock pads, lines and impact tests, usually by the maker's certified installer whose warranty you must pass through.</p>`,
  ex: `<p>11-428704: the first three bidders (ACCU, Diversified Landscape, Griffith) all listed a sub for Electric Service for Irrigation (item 69), bid $7,000–$25,000. 11-4C0034: Diversified listed Fortte Construction for Modifying Lighting Systems; the winner listed Siege Electric for 100% of item 60, $170,000 in the low bid.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>No power, no controller test or programming, no finish. Write: “120V power, conduit and final connection to controller(s) by electrical contractor. We provide the controller and coordinate its location.”</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-10, C-7 and C-61/D-12 classification pages; Caltrans 11-428704 Bid Summary item 69 and subcontractor lists (see Module 15); 11-4C0034 Bid Summary pages 4 and S1–S3."
},

{ h: "Water meter, POC, backflow, temporary water and sleeves",
  lead: "Irrigation starts at the point of connection (POC). Meter fees can be a large pass-through; sleeves and temporary water need a written owner.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Usually</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Water meter and capacity fees</td><td>Agency sets; owner or contractor pays</td><td>Capacity fees for a new irrigation meter can be large</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service line, main to meter</td><td>Agency or civil sub</td><td>Tapping a public main isn't landscape work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water service charges</td><td>Owner (private); prime (public)</td><td>Monthly bills through build and establishment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow preventer and enclosure</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td><td>Certified tester after install; pad by whom; freeze protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary water</td><td>GC (private); prime (public)</td><td>Hydrant meter deposit and rental, hauled water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves under paving</td><td>Landscape, before paving</td><td>Months before planting; mark ends</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Private job wording:</strong> “Water meter, meter and capacity fees, service line to the POC, and water usage by owner/GC. Irrigation work starts at the POC shown on L-1.” “Sleeves by others per our sleeve layout, installed before paving.” Otherwise price boring or sawcutting.</p>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>11-431064 (SR-905 Otay Mesa), SP 5-1.20E:</strong> two 2" Otay Water District meters, $210 connection fee each, one with a $118,247 meter charge (the other covered by credits). Contractor must “make arrangements and pay the charges”; Caltrans adjusts the LS if charges change. Bids for Water Meter Charges: $118,000, $148,000, $146,900; the winner was $667 under the $118,667 total. Water service: City $78.72/month + $7.053/HCF; Otay $240.85/meter/month + $5.84/HCF. Irrigation Water Service Charges drew $60,000, $60,000 and $553,400 (<a href="#m18-1">Module 18</a> works this job).</p>
<p><strong>Backflow:</strong> 2" Backflow Preventer Assembly on 11-431064 bid $7,000, $8,100, $9,000 each; Certify Existing Backflow Preventers (11-422004, LS) $275–$2,200. City Fire Hydrant Meter Program (K-24-1952 Appendix B): temporary use no longer than 12 months, 2½" swivel, reduced pressure principle backflow assembly required, refundable deposit.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>On a public prime bid, find the fee table and carry at least the listed amount plus your time to arrange it.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-431064 Special Provisions 5-1.20E and 5-1.20F; Bid Summaries 11-431064 items 46, 67 and 68, 11-430674 item 72, 11-422004 item 32; City of San Diego K-24-1952 Appendix B (Water Department Instruction DI 55.27, Fire Hydrant Meter Program), sections 3.2, 4.1 and 4.2."
},

{ h: "DigAlert (811), potholing and private locating",
  lead: "California law requires delineating the dig area and notifying the regional center before digging. In Southern California that is DigAlert, at 811.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Gov. Code</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Delineate</td><td>Mark the dig area in white; owners may refuse to locate an undelineated area</td><td>§4216</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notice window</td><td>At least 2 working days and not more than 14 calendar days before digging; the day you call doesn't count</td><td>§4216.2(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ticket life</td><td>28 days; renew by end of day 28 if work continues</td><td>§4216.2(d)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tolerance zone</td><td>24" each side of a mark; expose with hand tools before power equipment</td><td>§4216, §4216.4(a)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private lines</td><td>DigAlert doesn't locate on-site private irrigation, lighting or drains; hire a private locator</td><td></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Potholing</strong> (test holes, often vacuum excavation) costs time and money wherever your trench crosses marks. Price it and the slower trenching in tolerance zones (<a href="#m9-1">Module 9</a>).</p>`,
  ex: `<p>Caltrans Subsurface Locator (LS), 11-431064 SP 87-1.03X: certified operators, two locating technologies (one GPR or subsurface imaging), marks maintained to completion, GPS data for each facility. Bids $9,400, $11,000, $25,800 (item 65); 11-428804, $1,300–$60,819.75.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Gov. Code §§4216, 4216.2 and 4216.4 (text checked at california.public.law; official text at leginfo.legislature.ca.gov); Caltrans 11-431064 Special Provisions 87-1.01D(3) and 87-1.03X; Bid Summaries 11-431064 item 65 and 11-428804 item 98."
},

{ h: "Plant establishment and general conditions",
  lead: "Establishment is landscape work priced by time: often 90 days privately, hundreds of working days on Caltrans. General conditions are line items when you're prime and GC exclusions when you're a sub.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Establishment period</th><th class="num">Plant Establishment Work (LS) bids</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>City K-24-1952</td><td>90 calendar days inside 270 working days</td><td class="num">Inside the LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-428704</td><td>250 working days</td><td class="num">$10,000 – $300,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-431064</td><td>750 working days</td><td class="num">$95,000 – $153,900</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-430674</td><td>750 working days</td><td class="num">$153,000 – $510,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-4C0034</td><td>After 110 working days</td><td class="num">$10,000 (low bid)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>General condition</th><th>Real bid range (job, item)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Traffic Control System (LS)</td><td>$63,067.34 – $350,000 (11-428804 #6)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Construction Area Signs (LS)</td><td>$22,500 – $75,000 (11-428804 #5)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mobilization (LS)</td><td>$20,000 – $330,000 (11-428704 #70)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Job Site Management (LS)</td><td>$5,000 – $125,000 (11-428704 #9)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manage Solid Waste Generated by the Public (HR)</td><td>$81 – $345 per HR (11-428704 #18)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing; dewatering</td><td>Often inside other items / rare on landscape: exclude unless shown</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  exLabel: "Worked example · 90-day establishment, 20,000 SF park (practice numbers)",
  ex: `<p>13 weeks × 2 workers × 4 hr = 104 LH × $36.00 = $3,744; truck 13 × $60 = $780; plant replacement 3% of $12,000 = $360; materials $400. Total <strong>$5,284</strong> = $1,761.33/month. Weed abatement before and during (<a href="#m7-1">Module 7</a>); 1- or 3-year maintenance is its own line or alternate.</p>
<p>Subbing pattern: on 11-4C0034 the second bidder listed a traffic firm for item 3, 86% of item 4 and the sign items; on 11-422004 the landscape winner listed a sign company for traffic and an environmental firm for the lead compliance plan, water pollution control program and part of job site management.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Check who pays water during establishment, whether the period extends for dead plants, and whether retention waits for its end (<a href="#m16-1">Module 16</a>). As a sub, don't let “all work required for a complete installation” pull general conditions into your scope (<a href="#m10-1">Module 10</a> prices them for primes).</p>`,
  src: "City K-24-1952 Attachment A item 3 and 6-1.1 item 4; Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-428704 items 9, 18, 30, 70; 11-431064 item 31; 11-430674 item 40; 11-4C0034 item 31; 11-428804 items 5–6; 11-4C0034 and 11-422004 subcontractor lists; contract days from the Real Bid Lab data (realbids.js)."
},

{ h: "Bid day: scope sheet words, subs and markup",
  lead: "Copy the GC's scope lines into your estimate in order, answer each one, and carry every sub at a markup.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>NIC</td><td>Not in contract: nobody does it</td><td>Don't price it or exclude it as if it were yours</td></tr>
<tr><td>By others</td><td>Another trade or the owner</td><td>Coordinate; don't let your price depend on it silently</td></tr>
<tr><td>OFCI</td><td>Owner-furnished, contractor-installed</td><td>Price install, receiving, storage; not the product</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alternate</td><td>Priced add or deduct option</td><td>Price exactly that scope, separately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allowance</td><td>Owner-set amount for an undesigned item</td><td>Carry it exactly; add labor if material only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plug number</td><td>Placeholder until a real quote arrives</td><td>Note its source; replace fast</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Answer every line</b>Included, included by named sub, excluded, or NIC. Give the breakouts the GC asks for; GCs level line by line.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Get sub quotes early</b>Plans, specs, addenda and written scope 1–2 weeks out. Check license, status, bond, workers' comp; DIR registration on public work.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Read the sub's exclusions</b>A concrete quote excluding sawcut, testing and pump time moved those costs to you. Compare two quotes when you can; last-hour quotes are often incomplete.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Mark it up and list it</b>You pay, coordinate, supervise and warranty the sub. On public jobs list subs over one half of one percent of your bid (<a href="#m14-1">Module 14</a>).</div></li>
</ol>`,
  ex: `<p>C-10 quote $18,500 + coordination 8 hr × $55.00 = $440 + 8% markup $1,480 = <strong>$20,420</strong> carried. If the GC can hire that electrician direct for $18,500, ask whether they want lighting in your package at all.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Excluding has a price too: the GC adds a plug for it while leveling. If the plug exceeds your cost to include, your low bid isn't low anymore. Exclude what truly belongs to others. Decline cleanly: “Our proposal covers the irrigation, planting, fine grading and landscape drainage on L-1 through L-8. We are not including the retaining walls on C-5 or the site lighting on E-1.”</p>`,
  src: "Listing threshold: City K-24-1952 Instructions to Bidders item 12.1 (subcontractors over 0.5% of the total bid, per the Subletting and Subcontracting Fair Practices Act)."
},

{ h: "Scenarios: private GC, City lump sum, Caltrans prime",
  lead: "Same scopes, three contract shapes. The written answer to each line changes who pays when something goes wrong.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Private retail GC scope line</th><th>Right move</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Demo existing planters</td><td>Include with assumed thickness and unit price for extra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading</td><td>Include with the ±0.1 ft rough grade clause</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area drains</td><td>Include in planters; exclude storm drain connection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete mow curbs</td><td>Include</td></tr>
<tr><td>Site furnishings</td><td>Install from a written supplier quote, or install-only if owner-furnished</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landscape lighting</td><td>Sub to a C-10 at markup, or exclude after asking the GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Establishment 90 days</td><td>Own line</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>City park, K-24-1952 style:</strong> prime must hold an A, so you quote to primes. Split the LS yourself (irrigation, planting, soil prep, finish grading, stabilized DG, furnishings, establishment) and name what you won't do (walkways, curb ramps, lighting, fence, gate). Ask each prime whether 300-1.1 removals in planting areas are yours and price them as a separate line. Carry the DG lifts and tests, the 1% slope and RE grade approval, and 90 calendar days of establishment. State SLBE/ELBE status (<a href="#m2-1">Module 2</a>). A quote $8,000 higher that includes demo and DG testing can beat a lower one that excludes them.</p>
<p><strong>Halfway through a private job:</strong> grade 3" high, no sleeves, no controller power. With the three clauses (grade tolerance, sleeves by others, 120V by electrical) you get two change orders and a schedule extension. Without them, all three are yours at your cost. Document the day it happens: photos, dates, same-day email, before the extra work (<a href="#m16-1">Module 16</a>).</p>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Caltrans 11-4C0034</strong> (“Replace gateway sign, install landscape, hardscape, & accent lighting”; engineer's estimate $2,176,430): Hazard Construction won at $1,597,008.50, listing Marina Landscape for items 19, 20–49, 51, 52, 64, Siege Electric for item 60, a rebar sub for item 55 and a sign and traffic firm. Diversified Landscape (2nd, $2,026,050.15) listed Hazard for items 53–55, a traffic and sign firm and Fortte for lighting. FEC Future Contractors (3rd, $2,798,036.25) listed Siege for item 60 and a wall sub for part of item 53. The civil firm subbed the landscape; the landscape firm subbed the wall. Price decided it.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>To prime a job like this you need wall and electrical subs with written quotes before bid day. To sub it, get the planholders list and quote every prime.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A: Notice Inviting Bids item 6; 7-3.1; 300-1.1; 200-5; 801-2.3; 6-1.1 item 4. Caltrans 11-4C0034 Special Provisions (Notice to Bidders) and Bid Summary, pages 1, 4 and S1–S3 (bid opening 05/11/2023)."
},

{ h: "Mistakes that cost money",
  lead: "Each of these has cost landscape companies real money, and each has a one-line fix.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Silent on scope sheet items</td><td>Answer every line: In, Sub, Out, NIC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing demo buried in another item</td><td>Search specs for “remove,” “clearing and grubbing,” “in conflict with”</td></tr>
<tr><td>No rough grade tolerance</td><td>State the hand-off grade in every proposal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forgetting swell when hauling</td><td>BCY × swell = LCY; round loads up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Big-job unit price on a small job</td><td>Price small items from crew days and minimum charges</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMP install priced, upkeep not</td><td>Add inspections, repairs and removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical or walls without the license</td><td>Sub to C-10, C-8, C-29, or exclude</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sub quote used without reading exclusions</td><td>Read every line; ask what's missing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing not priced</td><td>Find “testing” in every spec section you price</td></tr>
<tr><td>No potholing or hand digging near marks</td><td>Price it wherever trenches cross utilities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assuming meter and water are the owner's</td><td>Read the fee tables; state it in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>No markup on subs</td><td>Mark up every sub; add coordination time</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`
},

{ h: "Inclusions and exclusions checklist",
  lead: "Mark each line In, Sub, Out or N/A for every bid, then copy only the lines that apply, in the GC's wording. Full proposal format: Module 13.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>If included (sample wording)</th><th>If excluded (sample wording)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Landscape demo</td><td>Removal and disposal of existing planting, irrigation and mulch in planting areas shown on [sheet]</td><td>Demolition of existing landscape and irrigation by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard demo</td><td>Removal of [item] (approx. [qty] SF, [thickness]") with sawcut at edges to remain</td><td>Demolition of paving, curbs, walls and structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trees</td><td>Removal of [qty] trees and stump grinding to [depth]</td><td>Tree removal and tree protection by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hazardous material</td><td>(never include)</td><td>Testing, handling and disposal of hazardous or contaminated materials excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rough grading</td><td>(rarely include)</td><td>Rough grade to ±0.1 ft of finish grade less amendment and mulch depth by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading</td><td>Fine grading of planting areas per [details]</td><td>(normally yours)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Import / export</td><td>Import of planting soil at $___ per CY placed</td><td>Import and export of soil excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staking and survey</td><td>Construction staking by licensed surveyor</td><td>Staking and grades by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing</td><td>Testing listed in [spec section]</td><td>Third-party testing and inspection by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP / BMPs</td><td>Install and maintain BMPs on [sheet] for [months]</td><td>SWPPP, QSD/QSP services, sampling and reporting by GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dust and sweeping</td><td>Dust control during our soil work</td><td>Dust control, water truck and street sweeping by GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landscape drainage</td><td>Area drains and pipe in planters to [point]</td><td>Storm drain, catch basins and connections by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention</td><td>Engineered soil media and planting in basins</td><td>Basin excavation, liner, underdrain and structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete</td><td>Concrete mow curbs per [detail]</td><td>Site concrete, walks and curbs by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG, pavers, stone</td><td>[type] per [detail], including [testing/binder]</td><td>Paving and pavers by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walls</td><td>Seat walls per [detail] by licensed [class] sub</td><td>Retaining and site walls excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Furnishings</td><td>Furnish and install [items] per [spec]</td><td>Site furnishings by others / install only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playground, fence, shade</td><td>By licensed sub per [spec]</td><td>Playground equipment, surfacing, fencing, gates and shade structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Lighting and controller power by licensed C-10 sub</td><td>120V power, conduit and connections by electrical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water meter and POC</td><td>Connection at POC per [sheet]</td><td>Meter, fees, service line and water use by owner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow</td><td>Backflow assembly, enclosure and test</td><td>Backflow by plumbing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves</td><td>Sleeves installed before paving when notified</td><td>Sleeves by others per our layout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilities</td><td>DigAlert and hand digging at marked utilities</td><td>Locating and repair of unmarked private utilities excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Establishment</td><td>[90]-day establishment period</td><td>Maintenance after the establishment period excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>General conditions</td><td>(only as prime)</td><td>Traffic control, mobilization of others, permits, bonds and temporary facilities by GC</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`
},

{ t: "terms", h: "Words to know",
  lead: "Module-specific terms. Everything else is in the <a href=\"#dict\">Bid Dictionary</a>.",
  terms: [
    ["Scope sheet", "The GC's list of what each sub's bid package must include."],
    ["NIC / by others", "Work nobody on the contract does / work by another trade, the GC or the owner."],
    ["OFCI", "Owner-furnished, contractor-installed."],
    ["Plug number", "Placeholder price until a quote arrives, or a GC's number for an item you excluded."],
    ["Incidental and supplemental", "Other-trade work essential to finish your licensed work (B&P §7059, 16 CCR §831)."],
    ["Clearing and grubbing", "Removing vegetation, roots, stumps and debris before grading; may hide demo."],
    ["Salvage / protect in place", "Remove and deliver to the owner / leave and keep undamaged."],
    ["Rough vs. fine grading", "Site shaped to the grading plan by the grader / planting areas taken to finish grade by you."],
    ["FG", "Finish grade elevation."],
    ["BCY / LCY / CCY", "Bank (in place), loose (as hauled) and compacted cubic yards."],
    ["Swell / shrink factor", "LCY ÷ BCY / CCY ÷ BCY."],
    ["Relative compaction", "Density as a percent of the lab maximum, e.g. 90% per ASTM D1557."],
    ["CGP", "State Water Board Construction General Permit, 1 acre or more of disturbance."],
    ["SWPPP / WPCP", "Storm water plan for CGP jobs / shorter plan for smaller jobs."],
    ["QSD / QSP", "Writes the SWPPP / runs it in the field."],
    ["BMP / REAP", "One storm water control / Rain Event Action Plan."],
    ["Track-out", "Dirt carried onto paved roads by tires; APCD Rule 55 requires daily removal."],
    ["Bioretention basin", "Planted basin with engineered soil media that holds and filters runoff."],
    ["Stabilized DG", "Decomposed granite pre-blended with binder, placed in lifts and compacted."],
    ["Mow curb", "Low concrete strip between turf and beds."],
    ["POC", "Point of connection where irrigation meets the water supply."],
    ["Hydrant meter", "Portable meter on a fire hydrant for temporary construction water."],
    ["Tolerance zone", "24 inches each side of a utility mark; hand dig first."],
    ["Potholing", "Test holes to find a utility's exact depth."],
    ["PEP", "Plant establishment period after planting, before final acceptance."]
  ]
},

{ t: "quiz", id: "m18", h: "Check yourself",
  lead: "Application questions. Keep a calculator handy.",
  qs: [
    { q: "A GC asks you to remove a 900 SF slab, 4 inches thick. How many in-place CY?", a: ["About 3.7 CY", "About 11.1 CY", "About 33.3 CY"], c: 1, why: "900 × 4 ÷ 324 = 11.1 CY. 33.3 is 900 ÷ 27, which ignores thickness." },
    { q: "You need 180 CCY of fill. Shrink 0.90, swell 1.25, 10 LCY trucks. How many loads?", a: ["18", "20", "25"], c: 2, why: "180 ÷ 0.90 = 200 BCY; × 1.25 = 250 LCY; ÷ 10 = 25. 18 ignores both factors; 20 ignores swell." },
    { q: "An 8,000 SF shrub area was delivered at walk height; finish soil must be 3\" below. Swell 1.25, 10 LCY trucks. How many export loads?", a: ["8", "10", "7"], c: 1, why: "8,000 × 3 ÷ 324 = 74.1 BCY; × 1.25 = 92.6 LCY → 10 loads. 8 forgets swell." },
    { q: "K-24-1952 300-1.1 puts sawcutting and removal of mow curb and pavers inside clearing and grubbing, and 300-1.4 pays it in the lump sum. As the landscape sub, what do you do?", a: ["Assume it will be paid later as extra work", "Ask each prime in writing whether removals in planting areas are yours, and price them as a separate line", "Ignore it; clearing and grubbing is the grader's"], c: 1, why: "No separate payment exists, so someone must price it on bid day. Make the split explicit." },
    { q: "A scope sheet lists 120V controller power and 12V landscape lighting. Best answer?", a: ["Self-perform both under the C-27", "Sub the 120V to a C-10; use a C-7 or C-10 sub for low voltage or confirm with CSLB before self-performing", "Leave both lines blank"], c: 1, why: "Line voltage is C-10 work; CSLB lists low voltage landscape lighting under the C-7, and the C-27 text doesn't mention lighting." },
    { q: "A GC scope sheet gives you “all SWPPP compliance” on a 3-acre site. What do you write?", a: ["Include it; you install the fiber rolls anyway", "Install and maintain the BMPs on the EC sheet for a stated period; exclude SWPPP preparation, QSD/QSP services, sampling, reporting, SMARTS filings and fines", "Exclude all erosion control"], c: 1, why: "The SWPPP belongs to the GC with a QSD/QSP. Own only the BMP work you can price." },
    { q: "You're bidding a Caltrans job as prime that includes $385,000 of structural wall concrete you can't build. What are your options?", a: ["Exclude the wall items on the bid form", "List a licensed wall subcontractor for those items with a written quote, or don't bid as prime", "Leave the wall items at $0"], c: 1, why: "A public prime can't exclude bid items. On 11-4C0034 Diversified listed Hazard for 100% of items 53–55." },
    { q: "A gas line is marked 18 inches from your trench line. What does the law require?", a: ["Nothing; the mark is outside the trench", "Expose the line with hand tools before using power equipment there", "Call DigAlert again"], c: 1, why: "The tolerance zone is 24 inches each side of the mark (Gov. Code §4216, §4216.4(a))." },
    { q: "On 11-431064 the listed water meter charges total $118,667 and Caltrans adjusts the item if fees change. How do you price item 67?", a: ["At least $118,667 plus your time to arrange the meters", "$0, since it's a pass-through", "About $118,000, like the winner"], c: 0, why: "Known fees are a floor. The winner bid $667 under them and gave that money away." },
    { q: "A C-10 quotes $18,500. You add 8 hr of coordination at $55 and an 8% markup on the sub. What do you carry?", a: ["$18,940", "$19,980", "$20,420"], c: 2, why: "$18,500 + $440 + $1,480 = $20,420. $19,980 omits coordination; $18,940 omits markup." }
  ]
},

{ t: "practice", id: "m18", h: "Practice assignments",
  lead: "Real documents, real calls and real takeoffs.",
  items: [
    { h: "Check licenses on CSLB", hrs: 0.25, d: "<p>On <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CSLB's classification pages</a>, skim the C-7, C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13 and D-6, D-12, D-34, D-49 texts. Then look up two San Diego landscape competitors on Instant License Check and list every class they hold. Which scopes in this module could each self-perform?</p>" },
    { h: "Responsibility matrix and checklist for a real job", hrs: 1, d: "<p>Download a current landscape or park bid from the City of San Diego <a href=\"https://www.sandiego.gov/cip/bidopps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bid opportunities</a> page, or use a GC invitation you received. List every scope that isn't irrigation or planting. For each: typically by, license, your answer (In, Sub, Out, NIC) and the sheet or spec section. Then fill out the inclusions and exclusions checklist for it and save it as your proposal template (<a href=\"#m13-1\">Module 13</a>).</p>" },
    { h: "Call for real demo and haul rates", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Call or check one concrete and asphalt recycler, one landfill or transfer station (e.g. Miramar Landfill) and one trucking company in San Diego County. Record rates per ton or load for clean concrete, clean soil, green waste and mixed debris, plus truck hourly rate and minimum. Rework the 600 SF patio example with your numbers.</p>" },
    { h: "Price the BMPs on a real Caltrans job", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Open <a href=\"#lab-11-428704\">Real Bid Lab · 11-428704</a>. Read special provisions section 13 (risk level 2, WPC manager, SWPPP by a QSD). Price items 10 through 17 with your costs, including maintenance. Compare with the six bidders and explain which items had fixed prices and why your numbers differ.</p>" },
    { h: "Take off stabilized DG from the City spec", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Using K-24-1952 Section 200-5, take off a 2,400 SF path with 320 LF of edging: compacted CY, loose CY, tons, binder minimum, test visits, every submittal and inspection hold. Price it with your rates. Check: compacted 29.6 CY, loose 35.6 CY at +20%, 49.8 tons at 1.4, about 597 lb binder at 12 lb/ton.</p>" },
    { h: "Who subbed what on 11-4C0034", hrs: 0.25, d: "<p>From the scenarios slide, list each prime's subbed scopes. Compute the wall items' share of the low bid: ($385,000 + $7,200 + $22,700) ÷ $1,597,008.50 = $414,900 ÷ $1,597,008.50 = 26.0%. In three sentences: should your division prime a job like this, and what would you need first?</p>" }
  ]
},

{ h: "Module 17 summary",
  lead: "Find every non-landscape scope, decide it on purpose, price what you keep and exclude the rest in writing.",
  body: `<ul>
<li>Every extra line gets a written answer: In, Sub, Out or NIC. Silence reads as included; public primes can't exclude.</li>
<li>C-27 covers landscape systems and grading <em>for</em> landscape (16 CCR §832.27); other trades only when incidental and supplemental (B&amp;P §7059). Walls, line voltage, mass grading, storm drain and playgrounds go to C-8/C-29, C-10, C-12/A, C-42 and D-34 subs.</li>
<li>Demo: SF × inches ÷ 324 = in-place CY; × swell = LCY; about 2 tons per CY of concrete. Find demo buried in clearing and grubbing.</li>
<li>Earthwork: LCY = BCY × swell; BCY = CCY ÷ shrink. Put the ±0.1 ft rough grade clause in every proposal.</li>
<li>Storm water: SWPPP is the GC's or prime's (QSD/QSP). Price BMP upkeep. Rule 55: daily track-out removal.</li>
<li>Hardscape: read the spec for lifts, binder, compaction and tests; small jobs don't get big-job unit prices.</li>
<li>Utilities: meter fees are a floor; sleeves before paving; DigAlert 2 working days ahead; hand dig within 24 inches.</li>
<li>Mark up every sub and read its exclusions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Next: <a href="#m18-1">Module 18</a> capstone · <a href="#m13-1">Module 13</a> proposals · <a href="#lab">Real Bid Lab</a></p>`
}
]
});
